Ported from UniXNU patches/mesa/turnip-darwin-kmd.patch (BSD-2-Clause-Patent),
commit "perf(turnip): wait for a fence up to 100 ms a call"; the pinned UniXNU
revision still slices at 1 ms. Applied after mesa-fuxi-smoke.patch, which
already clamps the slice to the remaining timeout.

turnip/darwin: wait for a fence up to 100 ms per kernel call instead of 1 ms.

A frame's fence wait was several IOKit calls (UniXNU i553: 258682 waits in
166 s, 97 % timed out). The kernel WaitFence polls until the fence retires or
the slice ends, so a longer slice only removes the user/kernel round trips; the
connection lock is still released between slices and the remaining timeout
still bounds the last slice.

--- a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_knl_darwin.cc
+++ b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_knl_darwin.cc
@@ -177,6 +177,6 @@
    for (;;) {
       uint64_t now = os_time_get_nano();
-      uint64_t slice = 1000000; /* 1 ms */
+      uint64_t slice = 100000000; /* 100 ms */
       if (abs_timeout_ns != UINT64_MAX)
          slice = abs_timeout_ns <= now ? 0 : MIN2(slice, abs_timeout_ns - now);
       NativeAdrenoAbiWaitFence req = {.fence = fence, .timeout_ns = slice};
